<AddOnInstructionDefinitions Use="Context">
<AddOnInstructionDefinition Use="Target" Name="P_HiLoSel" Revision="3.1" RevisionExtension="01 Release" Vendor="Rockwell Automation" ExecutePrescan="false" ExecutePostscan="false" ExecuteEnableInFalse="false" CreatedDate="2011-10-11T13:13:06.892Z" CreatedBy="Not Available" EditedDate="2014-09-03T14:33:39.141Z"
 EditedBy="PROCESS\Library" SoftwareRevision="v18.02">
<Description>
<![CDATA[High or Low Selector]]>
</Description>
<RevisionNote>
<![CDATA[See V3.1-xx Release Notes for details. Unmodified, up-rev only for V3.1-01 public release.]]>
</RevisionNote>
<AdditionalHelpText>
<![CDATA[Copyright © Rockwell Automation, Inc.  All Rights Reserved.

This instruction is used in an "Override Selector" control strategy.
It selects:
    the lowest of the (up to 6) incoming CVs (if Cfg_HiLoSel = 0), or
    the highest of the incoming CVs (if Cfg_HiLoSel =1)
and outputs sht selected CV value (Out_CV).

The unselected CVs are flagged to track the selected CV
(Out_CVnInitReq = 1).

The tracking value can optionally be offset by an amount
equal to the upstream PID or PIDE Gain*Error (Kp*E) to avoid
problems with ever-decreasing (if Low-Select) or
ever-increasing (if High-Select) output.

IMPORTANT:
    Inp_CV should come from the PIDE "CV" (in percent);
    Inp_Kpn should come from the PIDE "PGain" parameter;
    Inp_En should come from the PIDE "EPercent" parameter.

Scaling of the output of this block to CVEU can be done by a
downstream P_ValveC or P_AOut block.

This block also supports initialization from a downstream block;
the initialization is forwarded (with offset, if so configured) to
upstream blocks.

Refer to Liptak, Bela G., ed., _Instrument Engineers' Handbook:
Process Control and Optimization, 4th Edition,
section 2.28: Selective, Override and Limit Controls
(Shinskey, Shunta, Jamison and Rohr),
especially "Antireset Windup Overrides" on pages 341-342.
]]>
</AdditionalHelpText>
