</LocalTags>
<Routines>
<Routine Name="Logic" Type="RLL">
<Description>
<![CDATA[Select Highest or Lowest input CV as the output, and initializae the other CVs (with offset if needed).]]>
</Description>
<RLLContent>
<Rung Number="0" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Copyright © Rockwell Automation, Inc.  All Rights Reserved.
============================================================
P_HiLoSel : HIGH OR LOW SELECTOR
============================================================
Revision 3.1-01 Release  2014-09-03:  See V3.1-xx Release Notes for details.
V3.0-00  2013-06-30:  Initial Release (Part of Library of Process Objects V3.0, so Major = 3).
V3.0-01  2014-02-28:  Updated formal descriptive name and Instruction Help text.
V3.1-00  2014-06-30:  Added limiting of CVnPrev outputs.
V3.1-01  2014-09-03: Unmodified, up-rev only for V3.1-01 public release.
============================================================
This instruction is used in an "Override Selector" control strategy.
It selects:
* the lowest of the (up to 6) incoming CVs (if Cfg_HiLoSel = 0), or
* the highest of the incoming CVs (if Cfg_HiLoSel =1)
and outputs the selected value (Out_CV).

The unselected CVs are flagged to track the selected CV
(Out_CVnInitReq = 1).

The tracking value can optionally be offset by an amount
equal to the upstream PID/PIDE Gain * Error (Kp*E) to avoid
problems with ever-decreasing (if Low-Select) or
ever-increasing (if High-Select) output.

Refer to Liptak, Bela G., ed., _Instrument Engineers' Handbook:
Process Control and Optimization_, 4th Edition,
section 2.28: Selective, Override and Limit Controls
(Shinskey, Shunta, Jamison and Rohr),
especially "Antireset Windup Overrides" on pages 341-342.
============================================================
SHOW LOCAL STRINGS
============================================================
The STRINGs containing the text associated with each instance of this
instruction cannot be Input or Output Parameters, because STRINGs are not
 'atomic' types. (Inputs and Outputs must be SINT, INT, DINT, REAL, or BOOL.)

In order to make it easier to find and configure these STRINGs, this rung
was added.  THE JMP (and the LBL on the following rung)
MUST NOT BE REMOVED!!!

To view the STRINGs, go to the instruction instance (in LD or FBD), RIGHT-click
to bring up the context menu and select "Open Instruction Logic".
This rung will appear with the STRING values for the selected instance.
You may double-click the STRING values here to modify them as well.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[JMP(SkipText)[CONCAT(Cfg_Desc,Cfg_Label,Cfg_Tag) CONCAT(Cfg_EU,Inf_Lib,Inf_Type) ,CONCAT(Cfg_CVNavTag,Cfg_NavTag[0],Cfg_NavTag[1]) LOWER(Cfg_NavTag[2],Cfg_NavTag[3]) LOWER(Cfg_NavTag[4],Cfg_NavTag[5]) ,LOWER(Cfg_CV1_Label,Cfg_CV2_Label) LOWER(Cfg_CV3_Label,Cfg_CV4_Label) LOWER(Cfg_CV5_Label,Cfg_CV6_Label) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="1" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
CHECK CONFIGURATION
============================================================
This rung checks to make sure that the instruction is configured to 
HAVE at least one CV input.  If no, flag the configuration error.

Check to see that the Maximum CV (clamping limit) is greater
than the Minimum CV (clamping limit).  If not, flag the configuration error.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[LBL(SkipText)[XIO(Cfg_HasCV1) XIO(Cfg_HasCV2) XIO(Cfg_HasCV3) XIO(Cfg_HasCV4) XIO(Cfg_HasCV5) XIO(Cfg_HasCV6) OTU(Err_Has) ,LEQ(Cfg_CVMax,Cfg_CVMin) OTE(Err_Limit) ]OTE(Sts_Err);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="2" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[If a particular CV Input is not connected (doesn't HAVE), it cannot participate in
the selection (be USED).

The "USE" bits are available to Maintenance to allow a particular CV to be
ignored if that primary is disabled for maintenance.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIO(Cfg_HasCV1)OTU(Cfg_UseCV1);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="3" Type="N">
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIO(Cfg_HasCV2)OTU(Cfg_UseCV2);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="4" Type="N">
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIO(Cfg_HasCV3)OTU(Cfg_UseCV3);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="5" Type="N">
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIO(Cfg_HasCV4)OTU(Cfg_UseCV4);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="6" Type="N">
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIO(Cfg_HasCV5)OTU(Cfg_UseCV5);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="7" Type="N">
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIO(Cfg_HasCV6)OTU(Cfg_UseCV6);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="8" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[If any CV has its "Has" bit set (part of the strategy per Engineering
configuration) and has its "Use" bit cleared (CV not being used in
the strategy per Maintenance), set the "Maintenance Bypass"
Status, to show the breadcrumb for Maintenance attention.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[XIC(Cfg_HasCV1) XIO(Cfg_UseCV1) ,XIC(Cfg_HasCV2) XIO(Cfg_UseCV2) ,XIC(Cfg_HasCV3) XIO(Cfg_UseCV3) ,XIC(Cfg_HasCV4) XIO(Cfg_UseCV4) ,XIC(Cfg_HasCV5) XIO(Cfg_UseCV5) ,XIC(Cfg_HasCV6) XIO(Cfg_UseCV6) ]OTE(Sts_MaintByp);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="9" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
SELECTION LOGIC
============================================================
If configured as a High Select, skip to the High Select rung (below).
Otherwise, fall through to the Low Select (next) rung.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(Cfg_HiLoSel)JMP(HiSel);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="10" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
LOW SELECT
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
If configured as a Low Select, start with the Maximum CV
(configured) selected, and work your way down.

NOTE:  If two CVs have the same value, pick the lower-numbered one.
CVs should be connected to CV1, CV2, etc., inputs in PRIORITY order.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[MOV(Cfg_CVMax,Wrk_CV) MOV(7,Wrk_Selected) ,XIC(Cfg_HasCV6) LEQ(Inp_CV6,Wrk_CV) MOV(Inp_CV6,Wrk_CV) MOV(6,Wrk_Selected) ,XIC(Cfg_HasCV5) LEQ(Inp_CV5,Wrk_CV) MOV(Inp_CV5,Wrk_CV) MOV(5,Wrk_Selected) ,XIC(Cfg_HasCV4) LEQ(Inp_CV4,Wrk_CV) MOV(Inp_CV4,Wrk_CV) MOV(4,Wrk_Selected) ,XIC(Cfg_HasCV3) LEQ(Inp_CV3,Wrk_CV) MOV(Inp_CV3,Wrk_CV) MOV(3,Wrk_Selected) ,XIC(Cfg_HasCV2) LEQ(Inp_CV2,Wrk_CV) MOV(Inp_CV2,Wrk_CV) MOV(2,Wrk_Selected) ,XIC(Cfg_HasCV1) LEQ(Inp_CV1,Wrk_CV) MOV(Inp_CV1,Wrk_CV) MOV(1,Wrk_Selected) ]JMP(InitReqs);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="11" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
HIGH SELECT
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
If configured as a High Select, start with the Minimum CV
(configured) selected, and work your way up.

NOTE:  If two CVs have the same value, pick the lower-numbered one.
CVs should be connected to CV1, CV2, etc., inputs in PRIORITY order.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[LBL(HiSel)[MOV(Cfg_CVMin,Wrk_CV) CLR(Wrk_Selected) ,XIC(Cfg_HasCV6) GEQ(Inp_CV6,Wrk_CV) MOV(Inp_CV6,Wrk_CV) MOV(6,Wrk_Selected) ,XIC(Cfg_HasCV5) GEQ(Inp_CV5,Wrk_CV) MOV(Inp_CV5,Wrk_CV) MOV(5,Wrk_Selected) ,XIC(Cfg_HasCV4) GEQ(Inp_CV4,Wrk_CV) MOV(Inp_CV4,Wrk_CV) MOV(4,Wrk_Selected) ,XIC(Cfg_HasCV3) GEQ(Inp_CV3,Wrk_CV) MOV(Inp_CV3,Wrk_CV) MOV(3,Wrk_Selected) ,XIC(Cfg_HasCV2) GEQ(Inp_CV2,Wrk_CV) MOV(Inp_CV2,Wrk_CV) MOV(2,Wrk_Selected) ,XIC(Cfg_HasCV1) GEQ(Inp_CV1,Wrk_CV) MOV(Inp_CV1,Wrk_CV) MOV(1,Wrk_Selected) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="12" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
DOWNSTREAM INITIALIZATION REQUEST
============================================================
If a downstream block (secondary) requests that this block initialize,
go to that CV initialization value, and flag the initialization value
as the selected CV.

This will force all the upstream blocks (primaries) to initialize
to this same value (with offset, if required).]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[LBL(InitReqs)XIC(Inp_CVInitReq)MOV(Inp_CVInitVal,Wrk_CV)MOV(8,Wrk_Selected);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="13" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
CLAMP CV TO BE IN RANGE
============================================================
If the selected CV is outside the configured range,
clamp it at the appropriate range limit.

Identify the range limit (not the CV) as the
selected output -- to force initialization.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[GRT(Wrk_CV,Cfg_CVMax) MOV(Cfg_CVMax,Wrk_CV) MOV(7,Wrk_Selected) ,LES(Wrk_CV,Cfg_CVMin) MOV(Cfg_CVMin,Wrk_CV) CLR(Wrk_Selected) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="14" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
PUBLISH FINAL OUTPUT CV, SELECTION VALUE
============================================================
Selection above is done with internal Work registers, as the
data are changing through the above logic.

Send the final values out to the public parameters.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[MOV(Wrk_CV,Out_CV)MOV(Wrk_CV,Val_CVOut)MOV(Wrk_Selected,Val_Selected);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="15" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
OUTPUT INITIALIZATION REQUESTS
============================================================
If a CV is NOT selected and it IS being used,
send the initialization request to it.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[NEQ(Wrk_Selected,1)XIC(Cfg_UseCV1)OTE(Out_UseCV1Prev);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="16" Type="N">
<Text>
<![CDATA[NEQ(Wrk_Selected,2)XIC(Cfg_UseCV2)OTE(Out_UseCV2Prev);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="17" Type="N">
<Text>
<![CDATA[NEQ(Wrk_Selected,3)XIC(Cfg_UseCV3)OTE(Out_UseCV3Prev);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
